 terms of implementation and protocol changes. Additionally, transaction fees are currently small enough that the reduction in miner fees is unlikely to be a concern."]},{"tag":"h3","children":["Rationale for a block-level rule"],"id":"rationaleforablock-levelrule"},{"tag":"p","children":["The amount removed from circulation is a function of the block’s fees alone, so every node calculates it from data that it already validates. The coinbase transaction does not state the amount, and so no transaction field is needed. This lets the rule apply to coinbase transactions of every transaction version that is valid at NU7, and does not require the explicit NSM field of ZIP 233. ",{"tag":"sup","children":[{"tag":"a","children":["7"],"id":"fnref7:1","href":"#fn7"}]}]},{"tag":"p","children":["Because ZIP 236 ",{"tag":"sup","children":[{"tag":"a","children":["12"],"id":"fnref12:1","href":"#fn12"}]}," requires the coinbase transaction to claim its full total input value, the miner receives exactly ",{"tag":"math","children":[{"tag":"mrow","children":[{"tag":"mi","children":["𝖬"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝗂"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝗇"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝖾"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝗋"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝖥"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝖾"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝖾"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝗌"]}]},{"tag":"mo","children":["("],"attrs":{"stretchy":"false"}},{"tag":"mrow","children":[{"tag":"mi","children":["𝗁"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝖾"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝗂"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝗀"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝗁"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝗍"]}]},{"tag":"mo","children":[")"],"attrs":{"stretchy":"false"}}]},". A miner cannot remove less than the required amount by underclaiming, and cannot claim more than it is entitled to."]},{"tag":"p","children":["The contribution is rounded down once for the whole block. Rounding each transaction’s fee separately would, in aggregate, remove less than 60% of the fees: for example, two transactions that each pay a fee of 1 zatoshi would each contribute 0."]},{"tag":"h3","children":["Estimated impact on miners"],"id":"estimatedimpactonminers"},{"tag":"p","children":["Over 100,000 blocks starting at block 2235515, there were 316130 transactions. 60608 of them are categorized as ‘sandblasting’ transactions. The remaining transactions have an average of 5.46 logical actions (see ZIP 317 ",{"tag":"sup","children":[{"tag":"a","children":["16"],"id":"fnref16","href":"#fn16"}]},")."]},{"tag":"p","children":["The total fees paid to miners from those transactions, assuming the ZIP 317 regime, would be 87.86 ZEC. These 100,000 blocks were mined at the 75-second block target spacing and span about 86.8 days. Scaled to a 365.25-day year (420,768 such blocks), this is about 369.7 ZEC in fees per year."]},{"tag":"p","children":["If 60% of these fees are removed from circulation, that would be about 221.8 ZEC per year. ",{"tag":"sup","children":[{"tag":"a","children":["17"],"id":"fnref17","href":"#fn17"}]}]},{"tag":"h3","children":["Considerations for the Future"],"id":"considerationsforthefuture"},{"tag":"p","children":["If transaction fees were to increase, further modifications can easily be proposed to alter the 60%/40% split. Finding the optimal fee split may require an iterative approach involving adjustments based on real-world data and network dynamics."]},{"tag":"p","children":["Looking further into the future, there may come a time when the transaction fees become greater than the block subsidy issuance. At that time we may need to reconsider the 60/40 split. However, this will likely not be the case for the next 8-10 years due to forecasts based on issuance models and network traffic."]},{"tag":"p","children":["Further ZIPs may be proposed to remove ZEC from circulation in various ways to support network sustainability, including but not limited to:"]},{"tag":"ul","children":[{"tag":"li","children":["ZSA fees"]},{"tag":"li","children":["Fees and donations specific to decentralized applications"]},{"tag":"li","children":["“Storage fees” for any future data availability"]},{"tag":"li","children":["Cross-chain bridge usage / Cross-chain messaging"]},{"tag":"li","children":["Note sorting micro-transactional fees"]}]},{"tag":"h2","children":["Deployment"],"id":"deployment"},{"tag":"p","children":["This ZIP is proposed to activate with Network Upgrade 7. ",{"tag":"sup","children":[{"tag":"a","children":["18"],"id":"fnref18","href":"#fn18"}]}," It does not require the activation of ZIP 233 (“NSM: Removing Funds From Circulation” ",{"tag":"sup","children":[{"tag":"a","children":["7"],"id":"fnref7:2","href":"#fn7"}]},")."]},{"tag":"h2","children":["Reference implementation"],"id":"referenceimplementation"},{"tag":"ul","children":[{"tag":"li","children":["Zebra: ",{"tag":"sup","children":[{"tag":"a","children":["19"],"id":"fnref19","href":"#fn19"}]}," ",{"tag":"sup","children":[{"tag":"a","children":["20"],"id":"fnref20","href":"#fn20"}]}]},{"tag":"li","children":["Zakura: ",{"tag":"sup","children":[{"tag":"a","children":["21"],"id":"fnref21","href":"#fn21"}]}]}]},{"tag":"h2","children":["References"],"id":"references"},{"tag":"hr","children":[]},{"tag":"ol","children":[{"tag":"li","children":[{"tag":"p","children":[{"tag":"a","children":["Information on BCP 14 — “RFC 2119: Key words for use in RFCs to Indicate Requirement Levels” and “RFC 8174: Ambiguity of Uppercase vs Lowercase in RFC 2119 Key Words”"],"href":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/bcp14"}," ",{"tag":"a","children":["↩︎"],"href":"#fnref1"}]}],"id":"fn1"},{"tag":"li","children":[{"tag":"p","children":[{"tag":"a","children":["ZIP 200: Network Upgrade Mechanism"],"href":"?view=governance&section=zips&record=zip-0200"}," ",{"tag":"a","children":["↩︎"],"href":"#fnref2"}]}],"id":"fn2"},{"tag":"li","children":[{"tag":"p","children":[{"tag":"a","children":["Zcash Protocol Specification, Version 2026.8.0 [NU6.3] or later"],"href":"https://zips.z.cash/protocol/protocol.pdf"}," ",{"tag":"a","ch